meta {
  name: 02 - Direct RLS: user A reading their own transactions via PostgREST
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

get {
  url: {{supabaseUrl}}/rest/v1/transactions?select=*
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  apikey: {{supabaseAnonKey}}
  Authorization: Bearer {{access_token}}
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
}

tests {
  test("PostgREST returns 200", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });
  test("user A's own transactions ARE visible", function () {
    expect(res.getBody()).to.be.an("array").that.is.not.empty;
  });
}

docs {
  Sanity companion to 03-direct-rls-cross-user-empty: same JWT, same
  anon-key path, no user_id filter (RLS itself scopes the result to
  auth.uid() = user_id). Has to return real rows for test 03's empty
  result to mean anything -- otherwise an empty result in 03 would be
  equally consistent with "RLS is isolating correctly" and "this
  direct-PostgREST path is broken/denying everyone".

  Requires user A (the account behind {{access_token}}) to already
  have at least one transaction -- create one via POST /transactions
  through the backend first if running against a fresh account.
}
